  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Yes. Under the Manual's practice, other elements can neutralise a problematic connotation; the examiner should consider the trade mark as a whole.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/3/3/5",
          "quote": "When determining the potential connotation within a trade mark, the trade mark as a whole should be considered."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/3/3/5",
          "quote": "Other features incorporated as part of the trade mark can diminish the impact of the offending material and cast doubt as to how it will be seen and interpreted by the relevant consumer."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual illustrates neutralisation through Bavaria, quoting the Court's assessment that references to Holland neutralised the connotation with Bavaria.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/3/3/5",
          "quote": "‘….the reasonable consumer would not fail to observe the three references to “Holland”, which at least neutralise the connotation with the State of Bavaria’."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Neutralisation is not automatic: in its furniture example, the Manual says that the surname RUBELLI did not neutralise or change the Italian-origin connotation created by the Italian flag.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1",
          "quote": "The use of the Italian flag in this trade mark creates a connotation that the goods are of Italian origin."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1",
          "quote": "The inclusion of the Italian surname RUBELLI does not neutralize or change this connotation."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "For standalone geographical references, the Manual's stated confusion criteria include the absence of other elements or surrounding circumstances that would mitigate the risk of confusion.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1",
          "quote": "A trade mark containing a standalone geographical reference will cause confusion if each of the following criteria are met: Consumers would have reason to associate the goods/services with the geographical location; The association between the place and the goods/services would influence the purchasing decision of an average consumer of the goods/services. There are no other elements in the mark, or surrounding circumstances, which would mitigate the risk of confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The statutory test remains whether, because of a connotation of the mark or a sign within it, use for the particular goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion; if so, section 43 requires rejection.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s43",
          "quote": "An application for the registration of a trade mark in respect of particular goods or services must be rejected if, because of some connotation that the trade mark or a sign contained in the trade mark has, the use of the trade mark in relation to those goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    }
  ],
